meta {
  name: Send Remittance
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/remittances
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}
  Authorization: Bearer {{jwt_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "senderAccountId": "{{senderAccountId}}",
    "recipientAccountId": "{{recipientAccountId}}",
    "sourceCurrency": "USD",
    "destinationCurrency": "BRL",
    "amountMinorUnits": 10000
  }
}

docs {
  Sends 10000 minor units (= 100.00 USD) from the sender's USD wallet to
  the recipient's BRL wallet, converted via the mocked static FX table
  (MockExchangeRateProvider) and posted through the per-currency treasury
  wallets so each currency's ledger nets to zero independently.

  Prerequisites (run in order):
    1. Account / Create Account (Sender)
    2. Account / Create Account (Recipient)
    3. Wallet / Open Wallet (Sender)   — must hold enough USD to cover
       amountMinorUnits + fee
    4. Wallet / Open Wallet (Recipient) — must exist in destinationCurrency

  The Idempotency-Key header is a literal {{$guid}} — Bruno's built-in
  random-UUID runtime variable — so replaying this request manually doesn't
  collide with a previous send. Change amountMinorUnits or delete the
  header if you deliberately want to test the idempotency short-circuit.

  amountMinorUnits at or below 100000 (source-currency minor units, not
  FX-normalized) succeeds without a KYC profile; above that, expect 403
  (ComplianceRejectedError) — there's no HTTP endpoint to mark a
  KycProfile VERIFIED in this codebase yet.
}
